   "newText": "## Two-panel occupancy intersection\nWhen an ARC input is two equal-sized panels separated by a uniform divider row/column, and each panel is mostly a distinct nonzero background color with holes represented by 0, compare cells at identical coordinates. Output a panel of the same dimensions, placing color 2 where BOTH input panels are nonzero, and 0 wherever either panel has a hole. Evidence: task-d05f5e8a demos had same-size panels (colors 6/9 and 6/6 with zero holes), and the solved test used this cellwise overlap rule: top 1-panel AND lower 3-panel, output 2 for shared filled positions. Preserve panel orientation and alignment; remove separator from output. Do not output the union or the holes.\n\n## Paired-panel mismatch marker (task-f0199aba)\nInputs can have two equal-sized grids separated by a uniform divider column/row, with a shared background color and sparse, distinct marker colors on the panels. Remove the divider and output one panel preserving dimensions. For the demos in this task, output background color 3 by default; at each aligned coordinate, output the shared background color (e.g. 4) where BOTH panels are background cells (neither has its sparse marker). This is the key negative-space rule: the visible output background is 3, while jointly empty positions are recolored to the original shared background. Example test input: 12x27, divider 2 at col 13; left 13 columns (0-12), right 13 (14-26). Output 12x13, with color 4 precisely when left and right cells both equal 4, otherwise 3. Demos confirm: in pair1 (left 8/4, right 5/8), output 3 at positions both panels have 8, otherwise 8; in pair2 (left 8/7, right 4/7), output 7 wherever neither side has a marker and 3 at all other positions; pair3 separates with a full 5 row and copies the top pattern, recoloring its 7 markers to 3. For the test task, beware: some earlier attempts incorrectly treated any individual marker as 4; correct output is the complement of marker union, i.e. 4 only where both sides are 4. Alignment is direct, not reversed."
